{
  "title": "Symbolic Regression with a Learned Concept Library",
  "authors": [
    "Arya Grayeli",
    "Atharva Sehgal",
    "Omar Costilla-Reyes",
    "Miles Cranmer",
    "Swarat Chaudhuri"
  ],
  "year": 2024,
  "arxivId": "2409.09359",
  "abstract": "This paper introduces a novel method for symbolic regression, called L A SR, which uses zero-shot queries to a large language model to discover and evolve abstract concepts. These concepts are used to accelerate the search for compact programmatic hypotheses that best explain a dataset. L A SR outperforms state-of-the-art approaches on the standard Feynman equation task and is used to discover a novel scaling law for large language models.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "The Core Idea",
      "content": "The core idea of L A SR is to use <strong>abstract concepts</strong> to guide the search for <strong>programmatic hypotheses</strong> in symbolic regression. These concepts are discovered and evolved using <strong>zero-shot queries</strong> to a large language model. This approach combines the strengths of <strong>evolutionary algorithms</strong> and <strong>large language models</strong> to accelerate the search for hypotheses.",
